[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 6"""
 date="2026-10-07T20:36:53Z"
 content="""
Maybe I was mistaken about whether you have refs/remotes/hub-con/git-annex.
I don't think it matters; as far as I can see there's nothing here that
would be unexpected when there's a `git-annex forget` transition involved.

But what I wonder now is if the server ever replaced its git-annex
branch with the synced/git-annex branch that got pushed to it.
(Update: Based on comment #5 it has not.)

That needs git-annex to run on the server, and it may be that nothing
causes forgejo+aneksajo to run git-annex in this situation, and so it
leaves its git-annex branch at the initial `git-annex init` value.

The same could happen with a git remote that doesn't have git-annex
installed.

And that would leave synced/git-annex as the only place where new git-annex
branch information is being communicate. The problem with that being that
multiple clients can force push that, which can result in information sent
by one getting overwritten by another. While the clients would eventually
converge on a single merged git-annex branch via only synced/git-annex,
it might take some time.

I think that `git-annex sync` could deal with this better, by
checking if the remote git-annex branch's sha is in gitAnnexIgnoredRefs.
And if so, forcing a push to the remote git-annex branch,
using --force-with-lease to avoid overwriting any changes that might get
made to it at the same time.
"""]]
